            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around the attic access hatch. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

Air leakage was observed along the basebaords in the living room. 

Air leakage was also observed through three of the bathroom exhaust fans located on the main floor bathroom (with the laundry), the en-suite of the main floor bedroom and the main bathroom in the hallway upstairs. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 160 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.8 GJ. </airSeal>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation, as well as 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. 



Savings:

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation and 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation to the basement walls, you could potentially save $ 1,600 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 49.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Please note, the savings above assume the basement is kept at a temperature of 19 C (part of the standard conditions within the software). Currently, the basement does not have any direct heat (no ducts from the furnace), so it is heated by lost heat from the furnace and hot water heater. This may be part of the reason you mentioned the floors feel cold. By creating a duct into the basement for heating, this will help the floors to feel warmer. If you do plan to adjust the HVAC ducting for this, please consider insulating the bare concrete walls as mentioned above. </foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil-fired hot water tank. If you consider propane as a fuel source for the furnace in the coming years, I have modelled upgrading the hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher. 



Savings:

By replacing your oil-fired hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $ 140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil-fired furnace was originally rated at 83.4% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,800 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 39.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>COMBUSTION AIR

I did see a 5" combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the furnaceand hot water tank.

I recommend to install an electric damper on the combustion air pipe, to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the furnace or hot water tank is operating.



Savings:

By installing an electronic damper on the combustion air supply, you could potentially save $ 40 per year in energy savings.And this will help with cold air infiltrating into the basement making those floors feel cool. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



PELLET STOVE

Savings (with current oil furnace):

By using your current pellet stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 1,800 per year in energy savings. 



PELLET STOVE HEAT DISTRIBUTION

Your furnace evenly distributes warm air to the whole house. When you use your pellet stove, your furnace doesn't operate and you have localized heat that may or may not keep the rest of the house warm. 



One solution is to have a switch installed so you can run your furnace fan when you are using the pellet stove so you can distribute the heat evenly. You can contact your local electrician to have this installed.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



COLD ENTRYWAY

You mentioned the area by the front door generally gets quite cold. I believe this area becomes quite cool as there is no direct heat and it was mentioned the interior door is generally kept closed. The weathering stripping on the steel exterior door should be reaplced, as air infiltration was found around door seal. This may also be a significant reason why this area is cool, as it is not a large volume for air infiltration to replace the room with cold air rather quickly. 



One option could be to install a passive venting 'system' to allow the warm air to enter and mix with the warm air in the living room. This would include a small through wall opening on the top and bottom, on the wall between the entry area and living room. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

On April 1, 2019, the AEA launched the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (Program), providing up to $20,000 to help owners of older, less efficient homes achieve significant gains in the energy efficiency and home comfort. Completing an exterior wall insulation upgrade is a required first step to participate in this Program, this must be a minimum increase from the current insulation value by R10. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, there are other rebates available for reducing air leakage, upgrading windows and heating systems, and a bonus for reducing total energy use by a minimum 20%.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



If you are interested in adding addition main wall exterior insulation and accessing the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, please do not hesitate to contact me. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
